In the beginning, there were two rivers flowing from opposite mountains—the Silver River and the Golden River. Each year, the mountain spirits gave each river five sacred waters to sustain the valley below.

The Silver River could choose to share its waters with the Golden River. Whatever it shared would triple as it flowed through the Valley of Abundance. The Golden River would then decide how much of this abundance to return upstream.

For many seasons, the Silver River held back its waters, fearing betrayal. The valley remained parched, and both rivers diminished.

One year, a great drought threatened all. The Silver River, desperate, released all five sacred waters to the Golden River. Through the Valley of Abundance, they became fifteen.

The Golden River faced a choice: keep the abundance or honor the trust shown. Remembering its own thirst, the Golden River returned ten waters upstream. Both rivers swelled with life.

From that day forward, the rivers learned the dance of trust—sometimes giving fully, sometimes cautiously, but always remembering that abundance flows from reciprocity, not hoarding. The valley bloomed, and both rivers flourished together.

